# nvfbc-relay policy replay fixture
# Avatar 60 fps base with frame generation x3 (180 fps displayed), -src 60, b:vsync present, -lock -lag 75 -dejit. One benchmark test, no fade at either end. Batches of two or three members collapse to the real one, the lock holds at 1:1 and every present passes a real frame through, as at x2.
# units: microseconds. first value absolute, rest are deltas.
description Avatar 60 fps base with frame generation x3 (180 fps displayed), -src 60, b:vsync present, -lock -lag 75 -dejit. One benchmark test, no fade at either end. Batches of two or three members collapse to the real one, the lock holds at 1:1 and every present passes a real frame through, as at x2.
# What the RELAY did on this capture. The replay model is only trustworthy
# for a configuration where it reproduces these; the test checks that.
field_worst_run 0
field_long_runs 0
field_synth_pct 0.0
src_hint 60.0
extra_lag_ms 75
# rotation_inert: the vote decided 0 of 1439 batches here, and that is the right answer. This
# is DLSS frame generation, not Smooth Motion. The x3 subgen row measured the members: the
# later member of a two-member batch is a duplicate of the first on 84% of batches and a
# distinct frame on 16%, the middle of a three-member batch is distinct on 99.9%, and the
# distinct ones are the grabs that landed late enough to catch the next 5.5 ms flip. There is
# no rotating position for a vote to lock onto, and it does not need one: the output video
# measured against the FG-off 60x1 run shows the same content-step regularity to within
# noise, so whichever member keep-real keeps sits on the real frame's content grid. Field:
# 60.05 real frames a second out of 120 to 165 arrivals, lock 100%, 0 synth.
rotation_inert 1
# Replay 0.0% / runs 0 / worst 0 / re-seeds 0 against field 0.0% / runs 0 / worst 0: exact,
# and the same numbers as the 60x2 steady fixture. Any blend at all fails this one.
max_worst_run 0
max_long_runs 0
max_synth_pct 0.4
min_reseeds 0
